A||N||15||owenslee||5 spd Getrag vs. Izusu Shifter Identification||||5||
Z||000000||owenslee||04-13-2001||01:06 PM||||Can anyone offer an easy way to tell the difference between a Getrag and an Izusu 5 speed shifter once they are out of the car?<P>Any obvious physical difference or part numbers stamped in the framework?<P>Would they by any chance be interchangeable or do they differ enough in travel or cable mounting to prevent switching?<P>Any info is appreciated.<P>Kerry||||reg||5
Z||000001||rockcrawl||04-13-2001||01:40 PM||||I believe they are identical. They may have minor differences, but they are definately interchangeable. It is the 4 spd shifter that is different, it has the reverse light switch on it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||04-13-2001||02:41 PM||||The eaisest way to tell the difference in the 5-speeds is that the Getrag has a dip stick and the Isuzu does not.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||04-13-2001||02:52 PM||||I thought the dipstick was an '88 addition on the Getrag.  Do the pre 88 Gegrags also have one?  My Haynes manual says you have to check the oil by pulling the VSS on pre-88 Getrags.||||reg||5
Z||000004||mrgone||04-13-2001||03:01 PM||||the reverse light switch is on the shifter? excellent. my lights dont work, and i have the sifter console all cut up so what chould i look for in that mess?||||reg||5
Z||000005||HarryG||04-13-2001||03:10 PM||||Use a magnet. The Getrag has a steel cover plate bolted on the back.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tryxalon||04-13-2001||03:25 PM||||By your tests my 85 SE with a 5-speed Duke has a Getrag ... dipstick, metal plate, etc.  <P>I don't think you can tell the difference until you take it apart and compare the shift forks.  I also don't think it makes a large difference. <P>||||reg||6
Z||000007||owenslee||04-13-2001||03:26 PM||||Guys....I KNOW how to identify a Getrag or Izusu transmission...<P>I need to identify  a five speed SHIFT MECHANISM  that is in a box of parts to determine if it originally came from a Getrag or Izusu.<P>If the shifters are interchangeable as RockCrawl thinks, it would be helpful if some one could confirm.<P>Sorry if the original message was confusing, but I thought it was clear that this was a shifter question not a transmission question.<P>To clarify further...<P>This is the shift mechanism with shift lever / knob attached where the forward ends of the shift cables connect.  It  mounts on top of the console...<P>This is NOT the shift arms on the transmission.<P>Thanks.<P>Kerry<p>[This message has been edited by owenslee (edited 04-13-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000008||HarryG||04-13-2001||04:30 PM||||So much for speed reading!!!<BR>I'll bet Rodney Dickman will know.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tryxalon||04-13-2001||07:06 PM||||is there any difference in the shifters?? <P>I was under the impression there was a manual shifter and there was an automatic shifter. <P> There wouldn't need to be any difference on that end ... all the changes would be either in the cables or at the other end of the shift cables. ???????????????? ||||reg||5
Z||000010||rockcrawl||04-13-2001||10:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Sorry if the original message was confusing, but I thought it was clear that this was a shifter question not a transmission question.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No confusion here. Now if someone can just confirm my statements, you'll be set! For a minute there I thought I was the one who mis-read your question. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Indiana_resto_guy||04-13-2001||10:49 PM||||I'm sorry, I was one that miss-read your ?<BR>As long as it is a shifter from a 5-speed car it can be used for either transmission, it just don't care. None of them have an assigned shift pattern as that is up to the tranny. I'm not sure, but a shifter from a 4 speed may work as well.<P>IRG||||reg||1
Z||000012||Rodney||04-13-2001||11:06 PM||||The Getrag shifter is different than the Isuzu shifter. The area where the cables attach has some extra bracing. The pivot area for left to right movement has a tube instead of just the 2 holes in the plate and bushings. Besides that I don't remember any other differences. All 5 speed shifters interchange. The Getrag is slightly heavier duty. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||owenslee||04-14-2001||12:54 AM||||Rodney,<P>Thanks for a definitive answer.<P>If anyone possibly has a photo of the Getrag shifter, it would clear up the details Rodney describes.<P>Otherwise I will search for an illustration, possibly in the "P" parts book that shows the reinforcing or the tube you describe.<P>Thanks to all who responded.<P>Kerry||||reg||1
Z||000014||thomas_l||04-14-2001||07:31 AM||||I am installing my trans today and will be adapting (rigging) the shifter stuff because of this. The brackets and attaching linkage is slightly different but not unusable. The shifter parts up in the cabin ought to be identical since the cable movement on both ends should be the same. We'll see if I eat my words later today  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rodney:<BR><B>The Getrag shifter is different than the Isuzu shifter. The area where the cables attach has some extra bracing. The pivot area for left to right movement has a tube instead of just the 2 holes in the plate and bushings. Besides that I don't remember any other differences. All 5 speed shifters interchange. The Getrag is slightly heavier duty. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The big air space between my clutch and halfshafts used to be a Getrag 5-sp and the junkyard tranny going in is an Isuzu. I have already played hell getting the correct clutch assembly and am really looking forward to messing with the shifter linkage at the trans. I forgot how fun it is to finish up work that careless others gave up on!||||reg||1
Z||000015||owenslee||04-14-2001||11:41 PM||||Thomas,<P>Thanks for the reply and keep us posted on the outcome...<P>Inquiring minds want to know.........and all that.<P>Kerry||||reg||1
